.TH CHMAP_GET_PTR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_get_ptr \- get pointer to value by key (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

value_ptr = chmap_get_ptr(hm_name, key);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for chmap_get_elem_ref() that returns a pointer to
the value, or NULL when the map does not hold the key. Unlike chmap_get(),
it does not stop the program for a key that is not there.

The lookup key goes through the DECLARED key type of the map, in the
same way as in chmap_insert(3), so a key that the map stored through
that conversion is only ever found again by a lookup that makes the
same conversion. Both a literal and a computed expression are accepted.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Hash map to search
key      Key to look up
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the value if the key is found
Returns NULL if the key is not found
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Returns NULL when the map does not hold the key (it does not stop the
program)
Returns NULL when key is a NULL character pointer, which is not a
string
Stops the program when the size of the value does not match the size
of the type
Returns a pointer to the value, so that the caller can change the
value in place
The pointer is correctly aligned for the type of the value, with both
internal backends
A later insert, delete or resize invalidates the pointer
For a char* value type, the target of the pointer is const
(char *const *). The caller can read the stored string and edit its
bytes in place, inside the stored length, but replacing the pointer
itself is a compile error: the map owns the string bytes and keeps its
own accessor in step with them, so a replaced pointer would leave the
length of the accessor describing the earlier string. Call
chmap_insert(3) to replace a string value; it frees the old bytes and
copies the new ones into storage that the map owns
For a float or double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's
key equality contract (bitwise, except -0.0 canonicalized to 0.0)
For a long double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's key
equality contract (value-based, unlike every other key type)
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, int);
int key = 42, val = 100;
chmap_insert(map, key, val);
int* ptr = chmap_get_ptr(map, key);
if (ptr) {
  *ptr = 200; // Change the value in place
}

chmap_construct(names, int, char *);
chmap_insert(names, key, "hello");
char *const *sptr = chmap_get_ptr(names, key);
if (sptr) {
  printf("%s\en", *sptr);  // Read the stored string
  (*sptr)[0] = 'j';         // Edit its bytes in place: "jello"
  chmap_insert(names, key, "a longer replacement");  // Replace it
}
chmap_destroy(map);
chmap_destroy(names);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chmap_insert (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
